#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172853} :activity/id #uuid "deaf4c92-c8a0-5ffb-ac01-faeb66f39a39" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173121 :tutorial/content "A **Poisson process** is a mathematical model used to describe events occurring randomly over continuous space or time. A Poisson process is characterized by the following properties:\n\n- Events occur *independently*. In other words, one event's occurrence does not affect another event's likelihood.\n- Events occur at a *constant average rate*. The rate at which events occur represents the expected number of events per unit of time or space.\n\nWe encountered the exponential distribution in a previous lesson. The exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time $X$ between two events in a Poisson process with success rate $\\lambda$.\n\nWe have already seen that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ follows an exponential distribution, then the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThe exponential distribution is often considered the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. Recall that the geometric distribution models the number of trials before the first success in a sequence of independent Bernoulli trials.\n\nFor example, suppose a call center receives telephone calls at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute. If the random variable $X$ represents the amount of time between two consecutive calls, then $X$ can be modeled using an exponential distribution, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(3)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the time between two consecutive calls is less than one minute is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 1) &= \\int_{0}^{1}3e^{-3x}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-3x}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [- e^{-3}] - [- e^{0}] \\\\\n&= [- e^{-3}] + [1] \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-3} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.9502\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. If the call center receives calls at an average rate of $3$ per minute, then the probability of waiting less than one minute until the next call is received should be relatively large." :tutorial/id #uuid "3c557f1b-8f84-52d6-823a-8025e70b3114" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10786 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "86f3cc3d-b825-558e-89db-f7ded0fe1e84" :step/math-academy-id 231085 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171794 :question/id #uuid "fa51ca96-4ed2-585b-88e6-244b4d8dd66d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050018}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172642}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "b8119b3b-4fff-596e-94aa-acf0dc17ccff" :step/math-academy-id 231086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171795 :question/id #uuid "4c677102-9f65-58f2-8998-8353f81d84d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172635}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "3ef50142-79f2-5496-a8b7-805b3150108d" :step/math-academy-id 231089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172007 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171796 :question/id #uuid "4baa7bca-acfb-5f9b-a6b4-1fc4b949555d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf45d2f-484a-56ee-af3e-c14a63721c19" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172637}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Unbounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "5e0e069a-0219-5cb2-bbfa-c62742dcfbf9" :step/math-academy-id 231092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173122 :tutorial/content "The memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that the probability of an event occurring in the future is independent of the time already passed.\n\nFor a random variable $X$, the probability that the event occurs in the next $t$ units of time, given it hasn't occurred after $s$ units of elapsed time, is\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nSuppose you have a radioactive particle whose decay time $X$ follows the exponential distribution with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 0.8 \\text{decays/minute}\n$$\n\nThen, the probability that the particle will decay in the first minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 1) &= \\int_{0}^{1}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}0.8e^{-0.8x}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-0.8x}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-e^{-0.8} + e^{0} \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-0.8} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.5507\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose the particle doesn't decay in the first minute. What is the probability that it will decay in the next minute? We can answer this question using the memoryless property.\n\nBy the memoryless property, we have\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nTaking the complement of this event, we have\n\n$$\nP(X < s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = P(X < t) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $s=1$ and $t=1$, so\n\n$$\nP(X < 1+1 \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>1) = P(X < 1) \\approx 0.5507\n$$\n\nIn other words, even if the particle has survived for one minute, the probability that it will decay in the next minute remains the same as at the start of the observations." :tutorial/id #uuid "50a52f5a-aa3a-5935-9354-a35e097ff394" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10787 :tutorial/title "Modeling Using the Memoryless Property"} :step/id #uuid "48fbdce6-fb42-59ad-9328-65fe0616ba01" :step/math-academy-id 231095 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172858}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171797 :question/id #uuid "821b7a41-c7ee-54fc-9b12-b13330b109ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-16591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172533}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"} :step/id #uuid "a11e0baa-9867-5238-a3a8-1e70022bc1f2" :step/math-academy-id 231096}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172175}]}
